 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the highway law, in relation to redesignating the \"Army Captain Clayton Carpenter Memorial Highway\" to be the \"Major Clayton Carpenter Memorial Highway\"",
    "basePrintNo": "S964",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S964-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §344-e, Hway L",
    "lawSection": "Highway Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S964\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the highway law, in relation to redesignating the\n\"Army Captain Clayton Carpenter Memorial Highway\" to be the \"Major\nClayton Carpenter Memorial Highway\"\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill would redesignate the \"Army Captain Clayton Carpenter\nMemorial Highway\" to the \"Major Clayton Carpenter Memorial Highway\" in\norder to reflect Army Captain Carpenter's posthumous promotion to\nMajor.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one amends section 344-e of the highway law to reflect Army\nCaptain Clayton Carpenter's posthumous promotion to Major.\n\nSection two establishes the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nArmy Captain Clayton Carpenter, 31, was tragically killed on January\n15, 2014 during a training accident when the MH-60 Blackhawk he was\npiloting crashed near Hunter Army Air Field in Savannah, Georgia. A\nplatoon leader of for the 3rd Battalion, 160th Special Operations\nAviation Regiment, also known as \"Night Stalkers,\" Captain Carpenter\nwas a highly trained pilot, and revered by his fellow officers and\nmembers of the community.\n\nArmy Captain Carpenter and his family moved to Cortlandt Manor in 1992\nfrom Brooklyn, New York. Captain Carpenter attended Lakeland High\nSchool, where he was team captain of the boys' track and field team.\nWhile a student, he also participated in Mock Trial, student\ngovernment, Future Business Leaders of America and the Yorktown\nVolunteer Ambulance Corps. Following graduation, Captain Carpenter\nentered the United States Military Academy at West Point where he was\ncommissioned as a second lieutenant in 2005, receiving a Bachelor's of\nScience in Engineering Psychology.\n\nCaptain Carpenter's impressive military career included tours in both\nIraq and Afghanistan. Upon his passing, the community joined together\nto recognize this incredible local hero. Firefighters from across\nWestchester County came together to salute and provide honor guard to\nthe police escorted funeral procession as it entered the town.\nCaptain Carpenter's dedication to his country and community are\nevidenced through his military service and sacrifice. There is no more\nfitting way for the state to memorialize the honor and patriotism of\nArmy Captain Clayton Carpenter than naming a portion of the state\nhighway after this fallen hero.\n\nThis bill amends chapter 383 of the laws of 2016 to reflect Army\nCaptain Clayton Carpenter's posthumous promotion to Major.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
